1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,280
Hello everyone, and welcome to Two Cups English.

2
00:00:03,800 --> 00:00:06,700
I'm Mia, and Ben is here with me.

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:09,580
This podcast is for people who are learning English.

4
00:00:10,220 --> 00:00:11,060
We speak slowly.

5
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:12,970
We use simple words.

6
00:00:13,560 --> 00:00:16,400
And we repeat the important words many times.

7
00:00:17,320 --> 00:00:19,980
Today, our topic is sleep.

8
00:00:20,060 --> 00:00:22,520
And why sleep is not wasted time.

9
00:00:23,740 --> 00:00:26,360
Ben, how many hours did you sleep last night?

10
00:00:27,480 --> 00:00:27,920
Hi Mia.

11
00:00:28,460 --> 00:00:29,040
Hi everyone.

12
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:32,060
Last night, six hours, Mia.

13
00:00:32,680 --> 00:00:33,480
Not bad for me.

14
00:00:34,200 --> 00:00:36,080
But I was in bed for eight.

15
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:39,920
Two hours of phone, and then six hours of sleep.

16
00:00:41,000 --> 00:00:43,080
And I am tired now.

17
00:00:43,940 --> 00:00:44,520
What about you?

18
00:00:45,100 --> 00:00:45,800
How did you sleep?

19
00:00:46,980 --> 00:00:48,180
Seven hours, Ben.

20
00:00:48,640 --> 00:00:49,559
That is new for me.

21
00:00:50,280 --> 00:00:52,800
For five years, I was proud of one thing.

22
00:00:53,420 --> 00:00:55,980
I slept five hours a night.

23
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:57,140
Five.

24
00:00:57,220 --> 00:00:58,310
I said it at parties.

25
00:00:58,910 --> 00:01:02,180
"I only need five hours." People were impressed.

26
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:04,360
I was impressed with myself.

27
00:01:05,040 --> 00:01:06,980
And I was tired every day.

28
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:10,420
Five hours, for five years, Mia?

29
00:01:11,180 --> 00:01:12,280
And you were proud of it?

30
00:01:13,160 --> 00:01:14,240
And what happened?

31
00:01:14,980 --> 00:01:15,840
Why did you change?

32
00:01:17,280 --> 00:01:18,240
I got sick, Ben.

33
00:01:18,680 --> 00:01:19,220
Last year.

34
00:01:19,940 --> 00:01:20,920
Not very sick.

35
00:01:21,460 --> 00:01:23,160
But sick for a long time.

36
00:01:23,860 --> 00:01:25,320
Colds that did not go.

37
00:01:26,020 --> 00:01:27,580
Tired all day.

38
00:01:27,590 --> 00:01:29,830
And the doctor asked me one question.

39
00:01:30,590 --> 00:01:33,680
"How much do you sleep?" I said, "Five hours.

40
00:01:34,220 --> 00:01:38,340
I only need five." And she looked at me for a long time.

41
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:41,040
A long look from the doctor, Mia.

42
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:42,560
I know that look.

43
00:01:43,420 --> 00:01:46,700
Doctors do it before they say something true.

44
00:01:46,710 --> 00:01:48,180
What did she say?

45
00:01:48,280 --> 00:01:49,720
I want the exact words.

46
00:01:50,820 --> 00:01:54,020
She said, "Your body is not a phone, Mia.

47
00:01:54,490 --> 00:02:01,640
You cannot charge it to forty percent and run all day." And she said most people need seven or eight hours.

48
00:02:02,320 --> 00:02:07,220
And that the five-hour people are almost always tired and do not know it.

49
00:02:07,300 --> 00:02:10,340
Because tired becomes normal.

50
00:02:10,370 --> 00:02:14,489
"Tired becomes normal." Mia, that is our topic today.

51
00:02:15,240 --> 00:02:17,180
So here is our plan.

52
00:02:17,240 --> 00:02:21,100
In part one, we talk about our own tired years.

53
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:22,680
What five hours feels like.

54
00:02:23,620 --> 00:02:30,000
In part two, we ask what the brain does at night, and why sleep is not wasted.

55
00:02:30,040 --> 00:02:32,900
And I have a true story for that.

56
00:02:32,940 --> 00:02:36,180
In part three, we share simple ideas for a better night.

57
00:02:37,080 --> 00:02:37,820
Does that sound good?

58
00:02:38,800 --> 00:02:39,780
That sounds good, Ben.

59
00:02:40,320 --> 00:02:44,080
And to everyone listening, please do not worry if you miss something.

60
00:02:44,480 --> 00:02:45,480
You will hear it again.

61
00:02:46,100 --> 00:02:51,600
You can listen while you cook, while you walk, or in bed, resting, before you sleep.

62
00:02:52,380 --> 00:02:53,960
That is a good place for this one.

63
00:02:54,960 --> 00:02:55,519
Okay, Ben.

64
00:02:55,820 --> 00:02:56,340
Part one.

65
00:02:57,300 --> 00:02:59,340
You said you had a tired year too.

66
00:02:59,840 --> 00:03:00,240
Tell me.

67
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:02,500
I did, Mia.

68
00:03:03,200 --> 00:03:06,740
Two jobs, a baby, six hours on a good night.

69
00:03:07,600 --> 00:03:10,240
And I remember thinking I was just getting old.

70
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:12,960
I was thirty-four.

71
00:03:13,020 --> 00:03:14,040
I was not old.

72
00:03:14,820 --> 00:03:15,570
I was tired.

73
00:03:16,160 --> 00:03:17,680
And I did not know the difference.

74
00:03:18,680 --> 00:03:20,400
Tired and old felt the same.

75
00:03:21,320 --> 00:03:21,640
Heavy.

76
00:03:22,540 --> 00:03:23,880
Slow.

77
00:03:23,960 --> 00:03:24,640
No ideas.

78
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:26,280
Heavy.

79
00:03:26,360 --> 00:03:27,760
That is my word too, Ben.

80
00:03:28,580 --> 00:03:32,240
Every morning for five years was heavy.

81
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:40,820
I woke up, and the first feeling was not "good morning." It was "again." The alarm, the coffee, the fog.

82
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:43,740
I needed two coffees before I could speak.

83
00:03:44,540 --> 00:03:45,620
And the afternoon, Mia?

84
00:03:46,260 --> 00:03:49,700
Because the morning is one thing, but three o'clock is the real test.

85
00:03:50,260 --> 00:03:51,740
Everyone is tired at three.

86
00:03:52,380 --> 00:03:53,520
What was three o'clock like?

87
00:03:54,820 --> 00:03:55,840
Finished, Ben.

88
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:00,140
By three, I was finished, and I had four hours of work left.

89
00:04:00,720 --> 00:04:01,900
So I pushed.

90
00:04:01,960 --> 00:04:02,880
More coffee.

91
00:04:02,920 --> 00:04:03,510
More sugar.

92
00:04:04,180 --> 00:04:06,640
And at night, I was too tired to sleep.

93
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:07,990
Strange, but true.

94
00:04:08,680 --> 00:04:10,160
Too tired to sleep.

95
00:04:10,860 --> 00:04:12,700
So I looked at my phone until one.

96
00:04:13,200 --> 00:04:15,339
And then five hours.

97
00:04:15,420 --> 00:04:15,900
Again.

98
00:04:16,659 --> 00:04:20,280
"Too tired to sleep." Mia, I know that exactly.

99
00:04:21,000 --> 00:04:24,500
The body is finished, and the brain is still running.

100
00:04:24,560 --> 00:04:26,600
And we think sleep is the price of the day.

101
00:04:27,060 --> 00:04:29,839
Something we pay at the end, as little as possible.

102
00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:32,540
But sleep is not the price of the day.

103
00:04:33,140 --> 00:04:34,160
It is part of the day.

104
00:04:34,780 --> 00:04:37,560
It is rest, and rest is work too.

105
00:04:38,380 --> 00:04:39,680
Rest is work, Ben?

106
00:04:40,300 --> 00:04:41,880
That sounds like a contradiction.

107
00:04:42,280 --> 00:04:43,800
My boss would not agree.

108
00:04:45,420 --> 00:04:47,140
Your boss is awake, Mia.

109
00:04:47,260 --> 00:04:49,000
His brain is not.

110
00:04:49,060 --> 00:04:50,900
Nobody's boss sees the night shift.

111
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:54,410
But it is there, every night, for everyone.

112
00:04:55,220 --> 00:04:58,100
And a worker who never rests does bad work.

113
00:04:58,880 --> 00:05:00,340
You know that from three o'clock.

114
00:05:01,240 --> 00:05:02,680
Same for the brain.

115
00:05:02,740 --> 00:05:07,360
The night is when the brain does the work it cannot do while you are talking to your boss.

116
00:05:08,080 --> 00:05:10,080
So rest is not the opposite of work.

117
00:05:10,720 --> 00:05:12,460
Rest is the other half of work.

118
00:05:13,290 --> 00:05:18,940
"Sleep is part of the day, not the price of it." Ben, that is the sentence, I think.

119
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:21,860
So what happened when you started sleeping more?

120
00:05:22,180 --> 00:05:22,740
Did it help?

121
00:05:24,360 --> 00:05:26,280
Not in the first week, Mia.

122
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:27,540
That is the strange part.

123
00:05:28,400 --> 00:05:30,890
I slept seven hours, and I felt worse.

124
00:05:31,560 --> 00:05:32,230
More tired.

125
00:05:32,880 --> 00:05:36,120
Like the body was saying, "Oh, you are listening now?

126
00:05:36,240 --> 00:05:41,440
Here is everything I wanted to say." And then, in week two, something opened.

127
00:05:42,160 --> 00:05:44,179
Like somebody opened a window in my head.

128
00:05:44,980 --> 00:05:47,040
A window in week two, Ben.

129
00:05:47,570 --> 00:05:48,550
What did it feel like?

130
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:52,560
Because I want our listeners to know what they are waiting for.

131
00:05:52,640 --> 00:05:55,220
Like being fifteen again, Mia.

132
00:05:55,400 --> 00:05:56,800
I woke up before the alarm.

133
00:05:57,660 --> 00:05:59,380
I was not angry at the alarm.

134
00:06:00,260 --> 00:06:01,560
I had ideas in the shower.

135
00:06:02,060 --> 00:06:03,040
Small things.

136
00:06:03,060 --> 00:06:05,320
But I had forgotten they existed.

137
00:06:05,340 --> 00:06:07,920
And at three in the afternoon, nothing happened.

138
00:06:08,500 --> 00:06:09,060
No cliff.

139
00:06:09,440 --> 00:06:09,980
No sugar.

140
00:06:10,740 --> 00:06:11,800
I just kept working.

141
00:06:12,660 --> 00:06:14,279
That was the window.

142
00:06:14,340 --> 00:06:16,970
A normal afternoon, for the first time in a year.

143
00:06:17,780 --> 00:06:24,600
"Somebody opened a window in my head." Ben, that is exactly what happened to me.

144
00:06:24,980 --> 00:06:30,640
The doctor said, "Try seven hours, for two weeks." Week one was worse.

145
00:06:31,260 --> 00:06:33,399
Week two, the window.

146
00:06:34,080 --> 00:06:36,920
And I had forgotten what not tired felt like.

147
00:06:37,520 --> 00:06:41,180
Five years of tired, and I had forgotten the other thing.

148
00:06:42,150 --> 00:06:45,000
We forget what not tired feels like, Mia.

149
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:48,780
That is the danger of "normal." And there is something else.

150
00:06:49,450 --> 00:06:50,800
We are proud of tired.

151
00:06:51,330 --> 00:06:56,320
"I only sleep five hours." "I worked until two." We say it like a medal.

152
00:06:57,280 --> 00:06:58,540
Like a medal, Ben.

153
00:06:58,960 --> 00:07:02,740
Nobody says, "I only eat one meal a day," with pride.

154
00:07:03,200 --> 00:07:04,660
But we say it about sleep.

155
00:07:05,360 --> 00:07:07,500
I wore that medal for five years.

156
00:07:07,980 --> 00:07:10,080
And the medal was making me sick.

157
00:07:10,980 --> 00:07:12,240
Take the medal off, Mia.

158
00:07:12,860 --> 00:07:14,000
That is the first thing.

159
00:07:15,260 --> 00:07:16,600
Okay, so that was part one.

160
00:07:17,000 --> 00:07:18,040
Our tired years.

161
00:07:18,700 --> 00:07:25,520
Five hours and a medal, two jobs and a baby, too tired to sleep, and a window in week two.

162
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:27,460
Now, part two.

163
00:07:27,520 --> 00:07:29,040
What does the brain do at night?

164
00:07:30,060 --> 00:07:34,240
Mia, this is my true story, and it changed how I think about sleep.

165
00:07:35,140 --> 00:07:36,320
Changed how you think, Ben?

166
00:07:36,920 --> 00:07:39,040
That is a big claim for a story about sleep.

167
00:07:39,640 --> 00:07:40,000
Tell me.

168
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:41,420
What does the brain do?

169
00:07:42,500 --> 00:07:43,380
Okay, part two.

170
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:45,240
This is a true story.

171
00:07:45,980 --> 00:07:54,080
In 2013, a scientist named Maiken Nedergaard, at the University of Rochester in America, published a study in the journal Science.

172
00:07:54,780 --> 00:07:57,700
Her team looked at the brains of sleeping mice.

173
00:07:57,710 --> 00:08:00,080
And they found something nobody had seen before.

174
00:08:00,980 --> 00:08:02,020
Sleeping mice, Ben?

175
00:08:02,800 --> 00:08:05,520
I did not expect mice in a story about my bedtime.

176
00:08:06,020 --> 00:08:06,740
What did they see?

177
00:08:07,670 --> 00:08:09,760
They saw the brain washing itself, Mia.

178
00:08:10,490 --> 00:08:13,460
All day, the brain works, and work makes waste.

179
00:08:14,080 --> 00:08:15,970
Little pieces of used-up material.

180
00:08:16,670 --> 00:08:20,820
And during the day, there is no room to clean, because the brain is busy.

181
00:08:21,540 --> 00:08:25,630
But at night, when the mice slept, the space between the brain cells opened.

182
00:08:26,220 --> 00:08:27,560
Like streets opening.

183
00:08:27,600 --> 00:08:30,960
And a clear liquid came in and washed the waste out.

184
00:08:31,850 --> 00:08:34,360
The brain washes itself at night, Ben?

185
00:08:34,400 --> 00:08:35,250
Like a street cleaner?

186
00:08:36,180 --> 00:08:37,940
Exactly like a street cleaner, Mia.

187
00:08:38,589 --> 00:08:42,240
The streets open, the water comes, the rubbish goes.

188
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:44,580
And it only happens when we sleep.

189
00:08:45,160 --> 00:08:46,740
Not when we rest with eyes open.

190
00:08:47,190 --> 00:08:48,520
Not when we sit quietly.

191
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:50,100
Only in sleep.

192
00:08:50,820 --> 00:08:53,520
So five hours means the cleaning stops halfway.

193
00:08:54,070 --> 00:08:57,060
And you wake up and think in a brain that was not cleaned.

194
00:08:58,060 --> 00:09:00,900
Five hours, and the cleaning stops halfway, Ben.

195
00:09:01,700 --> 00:09:04,080
So what does a half-cleaned brain feel like?

196
00:09:04,500 --> 00:09:05,209
Is that the fog?

197
00:09:06,220 --> 00:09:07,500
That is the fog, Mia.

198
00:09:08,140 --> 00:09:10,300
The waste from yesterday is still on the streets.

199
00:09:11,080 --> 00:09:12,399
So the brain is slow.

200
00:09:12,960 --> 00:09:13,920
It forgets names.

201
00:09:14,400 --> 00:09:15,600
It cannot find words.

202
00:09:16,240 --> 00:09:17,920
It gets angry at small things.

203
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:23,040
Nedergaard's team even thinks that years of bad sleep let the waste build up.

204
00:09:23,180 --> 00:09:25,480
So five hours is not only a tired morning.

205
00:09:26,110 --> 00:09:29,160
It is a dirty brain, every day, for years.

206
00:09:30,260 --> 00:09:32,580
Thinking in a brain that was not cleaned, Ben.

207
00:09:33,240 --> 00:09:35,080
For five years.

208
00:09:35,180 --> 00:09:39,860
I was thinking in a dirty room for five years, and I was proud of it.

209
00:09:40,580 --> 00:09:42,040
And now the room is clean, Mia.

210
00:09:42,760 --> 00:09:44,980
That is the window in week two.

211
00:09:45,020 --> 00:09:46,030
The washing caught up.

212
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:50,200
Let me say what sleep is not, because I had it wrong.

213
00:09:50,280 --> 00:09:52,960
I thought sleep was like turning off a computer.

214
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:53,740
Nothing happens.

215
00:09:54,320 --> 00:09:55,240
The screen is black.

216
00:09:55,960 --> 00:09:58,220
Every hour asleep was an hour of nothing.

217
00:09:58,880 --> 00:10:01,160
But the brain is not off at night, Ben.

218
00:10:01,760 --> 00:10:02,780
It is not tired.

219
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:03,880
It is busy.

220
00:10:04,420 --> 00:10:05,120
Washing.

221
00:10:05,240 --> 00:10:05,780
Sorting.

222
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:07,340
It is the night shift.

223
00:10:08,060 --> 00:10:10,820
"Sleep is the night shift." Mia, I like that.

224
00:10:11,460 --> 00:10:13,840
Because it makes sleep a job, not a hole.

225
00:10:14,480 --> 00:10:16,080
And a job you do not need to do.

226
00:10:16,760 --> 00:10:17,579
The workers do it.

227
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:20,920
Your only job is to close the doors and leave the building.

228
00:10:21,920 --> 00:10:23,280
Can I add something about English?

229
00:10:23,820 --> 00:10:26,180
Because the night shift does something with words.

230
00:10:26,820 --> 00:10:27,680
With words, Ben?

231
00:10:28,420 --> 00:10:30,980
I did not know the night had anything to do with words.

232
00:10:31,640 --> 00:10:32,300
What does it do?

233
00:10:33,300 --> 00:10:38,060
When you learn a word in the day, Mia, it goes into a small, temporary place in the brain.

234
00:10:38,820 --> 00:10:40,120
A waiting room.

235
00:10:40,240 --> 00:10:43,500
And at night, during sleep, the brain moves it.

236
00:10:43,540 --> 00:10:46,580
From the waiting room to the long-term room, where it stays.

237
00:10:47,280 --> 00:10:48,720
No sleep, no moving.

238
00:10:49,480 --> 00:10:53,640
The words stay in the waiting room, and in the morning, the waiting room is empty.

239
00:10:54,990 --> 00:11:00,060
"The words are gone because you did not sleep." Ben, that explains so much.

240
00:11:00,740 --> 00:11:03,140
When I was learning Spanish, I had a bad month.

241
00:11:03,550 --> 00:11:06,380
I studied every night until one in the morning.

242
00:11:06,500 --> 00:11:07,520
Hard.

243
00:11:07,560 --> 00:11:08,700
And nothing stayed.

244
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:10,360
I thought I was stupid.

245
00:11:11,060 --> 00:11:12,860
I was only tired.

246
00:11:12,900 --> 00:11:13,500
And then, Mia?

247
00:11:13,980 --> 00:11:14,600
What changed?

248
00:11:15,580 --> 00:11:16,460
I got busy, Ben.

249
00:11:17,040 --> 00:11:17,870
I studied less.

250
00:11:18,340 --> 00:11:20,539
Twenty minutes at seven, and then I slept more.

251
00:11:21,060 --> 00:11:22,700
And suddenly the words stayed.

252
00:11:23,380 --> 00:11:25,920
Less study, more sleep, more Spanish.

253
00:11:26,520 --> 00:11:27,980
I did not understand it then.

254
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:29,200
Now I do.

255
00:11:29,680 --> 00:11:31,600
The night shift was moving the words.

256
00:11:32,400 --> 00:11:38,900
"Less study, more sleep, more Spanish." Mia, that should be on the first page of every language book.

257
00:11:39,720 --> 00:11:41,580
And it is the same for this podcast.

258
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:48,060
If you listen at midnight and sleep five hours, most of what we said goes to the empty room.

259
00:11:48,140 --> 00:11:52,069
If you listen at eight, and sleep at ten thirty, the night keeps the words.

260
00:11:52,880 --> 00:11:54,340
The night keeps the words.

261
00:11:55,200 --> 00:11:59,320
And the night does it for free, Ben, while you rest.

262
00:11:59,350 --> 00:12:02,400
The teacher that works for free while you sleep.

263
00:12:03,760 --> 00:12:05,700
Okay, so that was part two.

264
00:12:05,760 --> 00:12:07,460
What the brain does at night.

265
00:12:08,360 --> 00:12:12,120
We talked about the sleeping mice and the street cleaner.

266
00:12:12,130 --> 00:12:14,400
We said sleep is the night shift.

267
00:12:15,240 --> 00:12:17,880
And we said the night keeps the words.

268
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:20,960
Now, part three.

269
00:12:21,060 --> 00:12:23,020
Simple ideas for a better night.

270
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:25,400
Ben, you go first.

271
00:12:26,340 --> 00:12:28,060
Okay, part three.

272
00:12:28,100 --> 00:12:30,340
My first idea is about the evening, Mia.

273
00:12:31,080 --> 00:12:33,940
Because sleep is decided in the evening, not at night.

274
00:12:34,880 --> 00:12:37,600
My sleep was bad because my evening was a cliff.

275
00:12:38,480 --> 00:12:43,200
Laptop until eleven, then dark, then lying there with a brain full of email.

276
00:12:44,260 --> 00:12:46,020
So now the evening slopes down.

277
00:12:46,760 --> 00:12:47,980
Eight, nine, ten.

278
00:12:48,840 --> 00:12:51,350
Each hour a little darker, a little quieter.

279
00:12:52,130 --> 00:12:53,480
The evening is for rest.

280
00:12:54,650 --> 00:13:04,040
"The evening slopes down instead of falling off a cliff." Ben, my first idea is about the word "tired," from the body's side.

281
00:13:04,900 --> 00:13:08,080
Because the body talks, and we do not listen.

282
00:13:09,180 --> 00:13:10,440
Tired has a sound.

283
00:13:11,310 --> 00:13:12,160
Heavy eyes.

284
00:13:12,740 --> 00:13:13,360
A yawn.

285
00:13:14,140 --> 00:13:16,300
Cold hands at four in the afternoon.

286
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:18,220
A wish for sugar.

287
00:13:19,180 --> 00:13:20,220
Those are messages.

288
00:13:20,920 --> 00:13:21,700
Messages, Mia.

289
00:13:22,370 --> 00:13:25,520
And for years, I answered every message with coffee.

290
00:13:26,490 --> 00:13:32,810
The body said, "Rest," and I said, "Coffee." And after a while, the body stopped expecting an answer.

291
00:13:33,620 --> 00:13:35,940
The body stops expecting an answer, Ben.

292
00:13:36,880 --> 00:13:39,100
Mine stopped years ago.

293
00:13:39,140 --> 00:13:43,900
So now, when I feel the four o'clock cold hands, I do not drink coffee.

294
00:13:44,660 --> 00:13:46,800
I close my eyes for ten minutes.

295
00:13:47,560 --> 00:13:49,320
On the bus, at my desk.

296
00:13:50,080 --> 00:13:51,500
Ten minutes of rest.

297
00:13:51,960 --> 00:13:52,660
Not sleep.

298
00:13:53,340 --> 00:13:53,820
Rest.

299
00:13:54,600 --> 00:13:56,420
And the body says, "Oh.

300
00:13:57,060 --> 00:13:57,560
Thank you."

301
00:13:58,540 --> 00:13:59,920
Ten minutes of rest, Mia.

302
00:14:00,990 --> 00:14:02,820
My second idea is the nap.

303
00:14:03,779 --> 00:14:05,980
In many countries, people rest after lunch.

304
00:14:06,760 --> 00:14:08,520
Twenty minutes in a chair.

305
00:14:08,620 --> 00:14:09,670
Not an hour in bed.

306
00:14:10,200 --> 00:14:10,840
Twenty minutes.

307
00:14:11,770 --> 00:14:12,820
And the brain rests.

308
00:14:13,420 --> 00:14:14,640
It is not the big wash.

309
00:14:15,180 --> 00:14:16,500
But it is a small one.

310
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:19,780
A small wash is better than a dirty room.

311
00:14:20,780 --> 00:14:22,500
Twenty minutes in a chair, Ben.

312
00:14:23,090 --> 00:14:24,040
And not an hour?

313
00:14:24,920 --> 00:14:30,120
Because when I nap, I wake up at five in the evening, and I do not know what year it is.

314
00:14:31,620 --> 00:14:32,720
That is the hour, Mia.

315
00:14:33,420 --> 00:14:37,560
The hour takes you into deep sleep, and you wake up in the middle of the wash.

316
00:14:38,520 --> 00:14:40,120
Twenty minutes stays near the door.

317
00:14:40,920 --> 00:14:42,370
You rest, and you come back.

318
00:14:43,280 --> 00:14:44,080
So set a timer.

319
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:48,020
Twenty minutes, in a chair, not a bed.

320
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:49,180
The chair is the trick.

321
00:14:49,930 --> 00:14:51,100
The bed says night.

322
00:14:51,849 --> 00:14:53,320
The chair says rest.

323
00:14:54,200 --> 00:15:01,400
"A small wash is better than a dirty room." Ben, my second idea is about the phone in bed.

324
00:15:02,200 --> 00:15:04,920
Because the phone is the biggest street-closer.

325
00:15:05,760 --> 00:15:09,340
The phone is light, and light says "day" to the brain.

326
00:15:10,260 --> 00:15:15,000
And the phone is people, and people say "party" to the brain.

327
00:15:15,100 --> 00:15:18,340
A small sun and a small party, in the dark room.

328
00:15:19,140 --> 00:15:22,070
A small sun and a small party, in the dark room.

329
00:15:22,730 --> 00:15:26,380
Mia, that is exactly what my two hours of phone were last night.

330
00:15:26,420 --> 00:15:28,960
And I am tired now because of it.

331
00:15:28,980 --> 00:15:30,300
So where does your phone sleep now?

332
00:15:30,900 --> 00:15:31,880
In the kitchen, Ben.

333
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:33,720
From our evening episode.

334
00:15:34,300 --> 00:15:36,180
And my bedroom is dark by ten thirty.

335
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:38,840
Because the brain does not read clocks.

336
00:15:39,160 --> 00:15:39,980
It reads light.

337
00:15:40,660 --> 00:15:43,620
If the room is bright, the brain thinks it is noon.

338
00:15:43,700 --> 00:15:45,500
So make the night dark again.

339
00:15:45,580 --> 00:15:47,300
That is the whole trick.

340
00:15:47,350 --> 00:15:52,320
"Make the night dark again." Mia, my third idea is for the people who cannot sleep.

341
00:15:52,620 --> 00:15:54,180
Because some are listening.

342
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:57,020
They go to bed at ten thirty, and they lie there until two.

343
00:15:57,580 --> 00:16:00,350
And they hear us say "sleep more," and they feel worse.

344
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:01,980
What do you say to them, Ben?

345
00:16:02,800 --> 00:16:04,320
I say, do not try, Mia.

346
00:16:05,080 --> 00:16:07,900
Sleep is a walk to nowhere, like our walking episode.

347
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:09,390
No destination.

348
00:16:10,040 --> 00:16:12,600
The brain closes when it stops trying to arrive.

349
00:16:13,220 --> 00:16:15,500
So lie there, in the dark, and rest.

350
00:16:16,200 --> 00:16:18,480
Resting with closed eyes is not nothing.

351
00:16:18,980 --> 00:16:20,800
The streets open a little.

352
00:16:20,810 --> 00:16:24,160
And most nights, sleep comes when you stop chasing it.

353
00:16:24,250 --> 00:16:28,980
"Sleep comes when you stop chasing it." Ben, that is kind.

354
00:16:29,040 --> 00:16:30,480
My third idea is about the medal.

355
00:16:30,980 --> 00:16:31,640
Take it off.

356
00:16:32,060 --> 00:16:33,720
Five hours is not strong.

357
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:35,080
It is a dirty room.

358
00:16:35,620 --> 00:16:37,040
So tonight, choose a time.

359
00:16:37,600 --> 00:16:38,600
Ten thirty.

360
00:16:38,640 --> 00:16:39,520
And say it out loud.

361
00:16:39,910 --> 00:16:43,040
"Tonight, ten thirty." Not because you are a child.

362
00:16:43,420 --> 00:16:45,460
Because your brain has a lot to wash.

363
00:16:46,140 --> 00:16:51,200
"Your brain has a lot to wash." Mia, can I tell you about my worst night?

364
00:16:51,220 --> 00:16:52,480
Because it shows the cost.

365
00:16:53,100 --> 00:16:53,740
Please, Ben.

366
00:16:54,220 --> 00:16:55,040
The worst night.

367
00:16:56,020 --> 00:16:59,740
Two years ago, before a big meeting, I did not sleep, Mia.

368
00:16:59,800 --> 00:17:00,840
At all.

369
00:17:00,880 --> 00:17:02,600
I prepared until four in the morning.

370
00:17:03,110 --> 00:17:06,220
And I went into the meeting with a clean shirt and a dirty brain.

371
00:17:06,820 --> 00:17:09,360
And I forgot the name of my own project.

372
00:17:09,740 --> 00:17:10,460
In the meeting.

373
00:17:10,820 --> 00:17:12,080
My own project.

374
00:17:12,780 --> 00:17:15,480
I stood there, and the word was gone.

375
00:17:15,580 --> 00:17:17,720
You could not say your own project's name, Ben.

376
00:17:18,340 --> 00:17:19,880
That is the cost.

377
00:17:19,980 --> 00:17:23,660
All night of preparing, and the tired brain could not find one word.

378
00:17:24,140 --> 00:17:27,699
Because the waiting room was full, Mia, and nobody had moved the words.

379
00:17:28,539 --> 00:17:32,180
Now, before a big day, I stop at eight and sleep at ten.

380
00:17:32,750 --> 00:17:34,440
Same rule as our evening episode.

381
00:17:34,960 --> 00:17:36,540
And the words are there in the morning.

382
00:17:36,980 --> 00:17:37,440
All of them.

383
00:17:38,460 --> 00:17:39,269
And your last idea?

384
00:17:39,900 --> 00:17:41,880
My last idea is about rest, Ben.

385
00:17:42,420 --> 00:17:44,200
Because rest is bigger than sleep.

386
00:17:44,800 --> 00:17:47,840
Rest is any time the brain closes its streets a little.

387
00:17:48,560 --> 00:17:50,140
Sleep is the big one.

388
00:17:50,180 --> 00:17:53,100
But sitting with a cup and looking out of the window is rest.

389
00:17:53,660 --> 00:17:55,180
The slow walk is rest.

390
00:17:55,700 --> 00:17:58,400
Ten minutes with closed eyes on the bus is rest.

391
00:17:59,040 --> 00:18:01,120
Small doors, all day.

392
00:18:01,160 --> 00:18:02,380
And the big door at night.

393
00:18:03,200 --> 00:18:07,630
"Small doors all day, and the big door at night." Mia, I love that.

394
00:18:08,150 --> 00:18:11,960
And I want to say something to the parents, the nurses, the night workers.

395
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:13,860
The people who cannot choose their sleep.

396
00:18:14,660 --> 00:18:16,290
I had a year like that, with a baby.

397
00:18:16,880 --> 00:18:18,380
And the science is not a judge.

398
00:18:18,920 --> 00:18:19,580
It is a map.

399
00:18:20,380 --> 00:18:24,460
If you cannot have seven hours tonight, take the small doors.

400
00:18:24,520 --> 00:18:26,340
Ten minutes of rest is still rest.

401
00:18:27,260 --> 00:18:29,240
Some people are in a hard season, Ben.

402
00:18:29,320 --> 00:18:30,720
Thank you for saying that.

403
00:18:30,730 --> 00:18:32,300
And I think that is our message today.

404
00:18:32,960 --> 00:18:34,500
Sleep is not wasted time.

405
00:18:35,120 --> 00:18:40,520
It is the part of the day where the brain washes itself, sorts the words, and puts the day away.

406
00:18:41,440 --> 00:18:43,440
Tired is a message, not a medal.

407
00:18:43,980 --> 00:18:46,220
So tonight, close the doors early.

408
00:18:46,280 --> 00:18:47,260
Make the night dark.

409
00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:49,120
Let the body rest.

410
00:18:49,160 --> 00:18:51,160
And let the night shift work.

411
00:18:51,220 --> 00:18:52,400
Let the night shift work.

412
00:18:53,360 --> 00:18:58,140
Mia, let's repeat today's important words, slowly, because our listeners want to remember them.

413
00:18:59,320 --> 00:18:59,640
Sleep.

414
00:19:00,480 --> 00:19:02,280
To rest with eyes closed at night.

415
00:19:03,500 --> 00:19:04,640
Sleep seven hours.

416
00:19:05,720 --> 00:19:07,040
Sleep is not wasted.

417
00:19:08,300 --> 00:19:08,680
Tired.

418
00:19:09,640 --> 00:19:10,340
Needing rest.

419
00:19:11,360 --> 00:19:12,100
I am tired.

420
00:19:12,720 --> 00:19:13,840
Tired is a message.

421
00:19:15,220 --> 00:19:15,540
Night.

422
00:19:16,440 --> 00:19:17,140
When it is dark.

423
00:19:18,280 --> 00:19:19,940
At night, the brain washes.

424
00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:22,150
The night keeps the words.

425
00:19:23,460 --> 00:19:23,860
Brain.

426
00:19:24,700 --> 00:19:26,140
The brain washes itself.

427
00:19:26,920 --> 00:19:27,800
A clean brain.

428
00:19:29,140 --> 00:19:29,500
Rest.

429
00:19:30,400 --> 00:19:31,560
Ten minutes of rest.

430
00:19:32,400 --> 00:19:33,800
Rest is bigger than sleep.

431
00:19:35,200 --> 00:19:35,560
Sleep.

432
00:19:36,360 --> 00:19:36,800
Tired.

433
00:19:37,720 --> 00:19:38,060
Night.

434
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:39,400
Brain.

435
00:19:40,360 --> 00:19:40,780
Rest.

436
00:19:41,760 --> 00:19:43,070
Five words, and one number.

437
00:19:43,560 --> 00:19:43,920
Seven.

438
00:19:44,410 --> 00:19:45,560
And one big idea.

439
00:19:46,200 --> 00:19:48,400
Sleep is not wasted time.

440
00:19:48,460 --> 00:19:50,740
Now, Ben, do you have a question for our listeners?

441
00:19:51,520 --> 00:19:52,420
Yes, Mia.

442
00:19:52,500 --> 00:19:54,420
Everyone listening, here is my question.

443
00:19:55,120 --> 00:19:56,860
What time will you go to bed tonight?

444
00:19:57,640 --> 00:19:59,860
Not what time you usually do.

445
00:19:59,920 --> 00:20:00,320
Tonight.

446
00:20:01,270 --> 00:20:03,520
Write it in the comments in one short sentence.

447
00:20:04,230 --> 00:20:09,620
"Tonight, ten thirty." And if you want, add what you are moving out of the bedroom.

448
00:20:09,680 --> 00:20:10,110
The phone?

449
00:20:10,800 --> 00:20:11,380
The laptop?

450
00:20:12,239 --> 00:20:13,340
We read every one.

451
00:20:14,680 --> 00:20:15,800
After seven hours.

452
00:20:16,720 --> 00:20:17,880
After seven hours.

453
00:20:18,380 --> 00:20:21,600
And if this episode helped you, please subscribe to Two Cups English.

454
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:23,809
We make a new episode every few days.

455
00:20:24,250 --> 00:20:28,040
Slow English, simple words, and one true story each time.

456
00:20:28,540 --> 00:20:31,430
The full text of this episode is in the description.

457
00:20:31,460 --> 00:20:34,040
Read it tomorrow, after the night has kept the words.

458
00:20:34,510 --> 00:20:36,040
That is the best time to read it.

459
00:20:36,720 --> 00:20:38,340
Thank you for spending this time with us.

460
00:20:38,980 --> 00:20:40,280
Maybe you are in bed right now.

461
00:20:41,080 --> 00:20:41,300
Good.

462
00:20:41,860 --> 00:20:42,800
This is the right place.

463
00:20:43,640 --> 00:20:48,040
Finish this episode, put the phone away from the pillow, and let the streets open.

464
00:20:48,840 --> 00:20:50,680
The brain has a lot to wash tonight.

465
00:20:51,380 --> 00:20:51,660
Let it.

466
00:20:52,400 --> 00:20:53,300
You did enough today.

467
00:20:54,200 --> 00:20:55,680
Sleep is the rest of the work.

468
00:20:56,480 --> 00:20:57,720
This is Mia.

469
00:20:57,750 --> 00:20:58,560
And this is Ben.

470
00:20:59,190 --> 00:21:00,640
This was Two Cups English.

471
00:21:01,100 --> 00:21:01,930
Sleep well.

472
00:21:01,960 --> 00:21:02,920
We will see you next time.

